// Fixture: string_escape_invalid_sequence
// Rule under test: GDDL Spec v4, String Literal Escaping -- exactly two
// escape sequences exist (`\"` and `\\`); `\` followed by any other
// character is a compile-time error, not a passthrough. Confirms `\n`
// specifically -- a sequence that WOULD have meaning in many other
// languages' escaping schemes -- is deliberately rejected here rather
// than silently accepted as a literal 'n' or interpreted as a newline;
// the spec is explicit that control-character escapes are out of scope
// entirely, not an oversight to work around.

define Message
	text = string 40

Message BadEscape
	text = "line1\nline2"

// Expected:
//   Compile error against field `text` -- `\n` is not a recognized
//   escape sequence (only `\"` and `\\` are). Rejected at phase 6
//   (resolution/point-of-storage), check "string_escape", same phase
//   and general shape as string_length's own rejection -- a malformed
//   value is caught at the same point as any other type-safety check,
//   not deferred or silently passed through.
